<Prose
  title="Why Rounder is free (and how it stays that way)"
  description="Rounder's tools run in your browser instead of on our servers, which is why they can be free, unlimited and private."
  heading="Why it's <em>free</em>"
>
  <p>
    Most "free" online tools are free until they aren't: three files a day, a watermark on the export, a 500 MB cap, then a
    sign-up wall and a subscription. That's because every file you drop on those sites is uploaded to their servers and
    processed there, and servers cost money for every file.
  </p>

  <h2>Your device does the work</h2>
  <p>
    Rounder's tools run <strong>inside your browser</strong>. Modern browsers can compress video, edit PDFs and even run AI
    models locally. When you use a tool here, the page downloads a small program and runs it on your own computer or phone.
    Your files never travel to us.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No limits</strong>: we don't pay per file, so we don't need to ration them.</li>
    <li><strong>Private by design</strong>: we can't see, store or leak files we never receive.</li>
    <li><strong>Fast</strong>: no upload or download queue, especially for big files.</li>
    <li><strong>Works offline</strong>: once a tool has loaded, most keep working without a connection.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>So how do we pay the bills?</h2>
  <p>
    Hosting a website whose tools run on your device costs almost nothing. The few costs we have are covered by a small number
    of polite ads. That means no pop-ups, no fake download buttons and no autoplaying video. If an ad on Rounder ever
    looks like a button, that's a bug, and we want to hear about it.
  </p>

  <h2>What you'll never see here</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Sign-up walls or "create an account to download"</li>
    <li>Watermarks on your exports</li>
    <li>Daily limits or "upgrade to premium"</li>
    <li>Fake download buttons or redirect chains</li>
  </ul>
</Prose>
